Q: What is BeanFactory in Spring?

`BeanFactory` is the root IoC container interface.
It creates, configures, wires, and manages bean lifecycles.

In real apps you almost always use **`ApplicationContext`**, which **extends BeanFactory** and adds enterprise features.
Understanding BeanFactory = understanding Spring’s core bean container.

────────────────────────────────────────
Key responsibilities

1) Bean instantiation (from XML / annotations / @Configuration)
2) Dependency injection (constructor / setter / field)
3) Lifecycle management (create → init → use → destroy)
4) Lazy vs eager creation (depending on container + bean definition)
5) Scopes: singleton, prototype, etc.

────────────────────────────────────────
Simple conceptual usage (legacy style)

// XmlBeanFactory is deprecated — shown only for history
BeanFactory factory = new XmlBeanFactory(new ClassPathResource("beans.xml"));
MyService myService = (MyService) factory.getBean("myService");

Modern equivalent idea:
ApplicationContext ctx = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);
// or Spring Boot: SpringApplication.run(...)

Common BeanFactory impl underneath:
`DefaultListableBeanFactory` (core registry used by ApplicationContext)

────────────────────────────────────────
BeanFactory vs ApplicationContext

| Feature              | BeanFactory                         | ApplicationContext                          |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Role                 | Basic IoC container                 | Advanced IoC container                      |
| Extends              | —                                   | extends BeanFactory                         |
| Singleton startup    | Typically lazy (getBean on demand)  | Eagerly creates singletons by default       |
| Events               | No                                  | Yes (ApplicationEventPublisher)             |
| i18n MessageSource   | No                                  | Yes                                         |
| AOP / enterprise     | Limited                             | Full integration                            |
| Web support          | No                                  | WebApplicationContext, etc.                 |
| Modern usage         | Rarely direct                       | Standard (Boot uses this)                   |

Events note:
Spring events are **in-process** (same app/JVM).
Between microservices → REST / Kafka / RabbitMQ, not ApplicationEvent.

────────────────────────────────────────
When would anyone mention BeanFactory?

- Interview theory / understanding IoC internals
- Very lightweight custom containers (uncommon today)
- Knowing that ApplicationContext builds on DefaultListableBeanFactory

For Spring Boot interviews: say you use ApplicationContext; BeanFactory is the lower-level IoC API underneath.

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview one-liner

“BeanFactory is Spring’s basic IoC container for creating and wiring beans. ApplicationContext extends it with events, i18n, AOP integration, and eager singleton startup — and that’s what we use in modern Spring Boot apps.”
